[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) says "one issue, one fix queued". Its reports describe three problems.
The parser fix that is queued solves only the first one.

<Callout kind="warn">
Do not close #4471's reports when the parser fix ships. The other two problems will still be there, and the
reports are filed under the wrong cause.
</Callout>

## The problems

<ProblemTable ticket={4471} {issues} />

## How each report was placed

The counts are the desk's own view, as #4471's table builds it from the export:

1. **One id, one report.** The export pages overlap, so some reports come twice.
2. **By tenant, not by intake tag** (`causeOf` in `#lib/kb/causes`). Every tenant on file writes the day
   first. A report sent through a booking-v3 form, which prints `MM/DD/YYYY` under its date field, is
   `hint`. Everything else is `parser`.
3. **One-day moves.** The desk's notes list reports whose date moved by one day, for example a Lisbon
   client who got Tuesday and a Toronto client who got Monday for the same meeting. A month/day swap never
   does that, so they are `shift`. Following the desk's rule, they stay counted where they are filed.

The intake tag is a guess from the reporter's browser. It disagrees with the tenant in both directions,
so it does not count reports.

## Why it was one ticket

Decision [0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject) merges on the subject line. A subject says what
the reporter saw ("wrong day on the invite"), never why, so all three problems landed in #4471. The #4471 table counts the one-day moves as
`parser`, and it shows `hint` as "no ticket of its own". Each problem now has its own ticket, linked above.

